+# Fisher science completion review: 6 October 2026
+
+**Verdict: ACCEPT for the inspected scientific implementation and language evidence after repair 3.** The historical P1 below is resolved. The earlier pending paired-rerun note is superseded by the inspected post-repair `paired-after-repair3.log`, which reports `LOGISTIC_TWINS_SCIENCE_OK`, and the final saved `science.json`. The parent owns overall closure. Independent advice; read-only review of the exact logistic-twins roots and declared baselines. No fits, tests, installs, code edits or commits were run by this reviewer. Parent-run logs were inspected, not inferred from file presence. Documentation builds remain outside this verdict.
+
+## Historical finding: resolved
+
+**[P1, RESOLVED] Keep representable slope interval widths when predictor scaling makes the variance underflow.** Julia `src/GroundTruth.jl:119–128` computes the slope variance as `centered[2,2] / s / s`, then takes its square root. For tied data `x=(-1e200,0,0,1e200), y=(0,0,1,0)`, the finite MLE is `alpha=-log(3), beta=0`. The slope SE is `sqrt(8/3)/1e200`, which is representable, but its squared variance is not. The parent-run targeted reproduction confirmed a reported beta interval `(0,0)` against expected endpoints `±3.2006077842368794e-200` (`.unlazy/logistic-twins/evidence/large-scale-red.log`). This is false certainty rather than an interval-only failure.
+
+Repair 3 computes slope SE as `sqrt(C[2,2])/abs(s)` before interval width (`src/GroundTruth.jl:123–134`). This is the correct transformation and matches the R approach. New regressions check both 90% and 95% intervals at `s=1e200` and `s=1e-160`, require positive widths, and compare endpoints using `atol=0, rtol=1e-12` (`test/logistic_contract.jl:53–63`). Those assertions cannot admit the original collapsed interval through an absolute tolerance. The genuine `s=1e-310` interval-overflow case retains finite points and alpha's interval; the separate analytic raw-coefficient overflow case still rejects points (`65–75`).
+
+The latest inspected parent-run language log reports 158/158 frozen-fit and Wald-interval assertions and 38/38 existence/input/iteration assertions (`.unlazy/logistic-twins/evidence/GroundTruth.jl-sh-logistic.log`). The repair-3 gate log reports G3, G4 and G5 exit 0 with matched expectations (`.unlazy/logistic-twins/evidence/julia-gates-repair3.log`). No additional targeted numerical run is required for this finding.
+
+## Findings that support the bounded implementation
+
+- The complete/quasi classification is correct for an intercept and one numeric predictor with both response classes: disjoint class supports imply complete separation, touching supports with predictor variation imply quasi separation, strict overlap plus full rank gives a unique finite MLE. Julia `src/GroundTruth.jl:169–174` and R `R/groundtruth.R:784–789` use exact comparisons. The singleton/tied finite case is exercised in Julia `test/logistic_contract.jl:41–43` and R `tests/testthat/test-logistic.R:41–45`.
+- Julia's score, information, stable tails, positive-definite solve, guarded Newton steps and recomputed convergence checks match the contract (`src/GroundTruth.jl:84–106,176–222`). A small damped step alone cannot pass. R uses native `stats::glm` and independently checks the final standardized score and predictor correction, with no Julia polishing (`R/groundtruth.R:798–818`). Native covariance and warnings remain separate from declared final-information Wald inference (`823–880`).
+- Full covariance transformation and negative-scale slope endpoint reversal are checked on the shifted analytic case (`test/logistic_contract.jl:44–50`; R `tests/testthat/test-logistic.R:48–60`). Both engines use normal quantiles for logistic intervals; the unchanged Gaussian path uses Student t (`src/GroundTruth.jl:80–82`; R `R/groundtruth.R:729–734`). Gaussian wrong-normal and SSE/n controls are present in Julia `test/logistic_controls.jl:81–92`.
+- The paired checker imports only selected scalar functions from the retained earlier audit, independently of production helpers (`scripts/compare_logistic.py:7–13`). Read-only `cmp` confirmed the retained `audit-verify.py` is byte-identical to the earlier audit's `verify.py`. Its checker reconstructs loss, score and information and enforces analytic coefficients/SE/endpoints (`.unlazy/logistic-twins/source/audit-verify.py:12–20,36–65`). The observed science report records 14 accepted engine fits across seven finite fixtures, five rejected designs, and worst paired coefficient difference `6.870948254800169e-11` (`.unlazy/logistic-twins/evidence/science.json:3–14`).
+- Wrong-answer controls cover sign flips, target swaps, missing/duplicate targets, initial-information intervals and changed fixture bytes, while row permutations pass (`scripts/compare_logistic.py:71–95`; `science.json:16–23`). Four-attempt accounting preserves three accepted points, two usable intervals, conditional coverage 1/2 and coverage per attempt 1/4 (`test/logistic_controls.jl:59–75`). Retained language logs record the language, compatibility and accounting groups before the historical defect, and the repair-3 gate receipt records their successful rerun after the fix.
+
+## Limits
+
+The retained Python oracle uses `p*(1-p)` and `y-p` (`audit-verify.py:15–17`); it is independent and useful on the frozen ordinary fixtures, but does not validate extreme-logit fits. The separate fixed-point helper tests at logits 40/700 support the implemented tail identities, not universal optimizer robustness at those logits. Agreement here establishes numerical agreement on declared one-predictor Bernoulli fixtures with unit weights, not repeated-sampling interval calibration, fitted mixed models or package-wide parity. The candidate documentation states those limits (`docs/src/validation.md:28–33,43–48`).
+
+**Assessment:** Independent technical review, whole saved draft; self-assessment and exact-draft completion validation are recorded separately. The source, language evidence and inspected post-repair paired receipt support the bounded science verdict. The parent owns overall closure and documentation acceptance.
